From: Antigravity Autonomous Agent <engineering@lenovo-d330-fix.local>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/i915/dsi: full panel power-off settle on resume for the Lenovo IdeaPad D330 panel

The Lenovo IdeaPad D330-10IGL (82H0) panel stays black after S3 resume, while
unbind/bind (full PCI re-probe) always lights it. Phase-48 iteration 1 proved
the resume enable path can run the reload path's fresh-device branch
step-for-step and still stay black (RESUME_FIX_LEDGER row 30), which leaves
the panel-side power state as the remaining resume-vs-reload difference: the
reload path tears the panel power down exactly like this driver's disable
path (assert reset, wait the VBT panel off delay, rails off) and lets the
power domain settle for the unbind/bind gap before the enable path powers it
back up, while the resume path powers the panel straight back up on top of
its retained state.

DMI-gated to 82H0, at the very top of intel_dsi_pre_enable() (before
intel_dsi_wait_panel_power_cycle() and the PLL work), and only inside the
power-domain init/resume window (display->power.domains.init_wakeref is held
from intel_power_domains_resume() until intel_power_domains_enable(), which
brackets the system-resume display re-commit; plain modesets run long after
that window, and on the D330 cold boot runs no encoder hooks at all because
i915 fast-boots on the GOP handover), run the same teardown the reload path
produces: MIPI_SEQ_ASSERT_RESET, the VBT panel off delay, MIPI_SEQ_POWER_OFF,
then a reload-scale 1500 ms settle. The existing pre-enable flow then powers
the panel back up: POWER_ON, panel_on_delay, DEASSERT_RESET, INIT_OTP,
DISPLAY_ON.

Sequencing only: VBT sequences the driver already runs elsewhere plus delays;
no operational register values are written.

Signed-off-by: Antigravity Autonomous Agent <engineering@lenovo-d330-fix.local>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/vlv_dsi.c | 48 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 48 insertions(+)
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/vlv_dsi.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/i915/display/vlv_dsi.c
@@ -717,6 +717,24 @@
  */
 
 /*
+ * Lenovo IdeaPad D330-10IGL (DMI product name 82H0): Gemini Lake DSI panel
+ * machine. The gate is computed once because the DSI enable path is hot and
+ * dmi_match() would otherwise scan the DMI table on every encoder enable.
+ */
+static bool d330_82h0_panel(void)
+{
+	static bool dmi_checked;
+	static bool is_d330_82h0;
+
+	if (!dmi_checked) {
+		is_d330_82h0 = dmi_match(DMI_PRODUCT_NAME, "82H0");
+		dmi_checked = true;
+	}
+
+	return is_d330_82h0;
+}
+
+/*
  * DSI port enable has to be done before pipe and plane enable, so we do it in
  * the pre_enable hook instead of the enable hook.
  */
@@ -733,5 +751,35 @@
 	bool glk_cold_boot = false;
 
 	drm_dbg_kms(display->drm, "\n");
 
+	/*
+	 * Lenovo IdeaPad D330-10IGL (82H0): the reload path (unbind/bind,
+	 * always lights the panel) tears the panel power down with the same
+	 * sequences the driver's disable path runs (assert reset, wait the
+	 * VBT panel off delay, rails off) and lets the power settle for the
+	 * unbind/bind gap before the enable path powers it back up. Over S3
+	 * resume the enable path instead powers the panel straight back up
+	 * on top of its retained state, and the panel stays black (the
+	 * iteration-1 fresh-device branch alone was not enough,
+	 * RESUME_FIX_LEDGER row 30). Replicate the reload teardown here, at
+	 * the top of the enable path, in the resume window only:
+	 * init_wakeref is held from intel_power_domains_resume() until
+	 * intel_power_domains_enable(), which brackets the system-resume
+	 * display re-commit; plain modesets run long after that window, and
+	 * the D330 cold boot runs no encoder hooks at all (i915 fast-boots
+	 * on the GOP handover).
+	 *
+	 * Sequencing only: VBT sequences plus settle delays; no operational
+	 * register values are written.
+	 */
+	if (d330_82h0_panel() &&
+	    display->power.domains.init_wakeref) {
+		drm_info(display->drm,
+			 "[D330 82H0] resume: panel power-off settle before DSI init\n");
+		intel_dsi_vbt_exec_sequence(intel_dsi, MIPI_SEQ_ASSERT_RESET);
+		msleep(intel_dsi->panel_off_delay);
+		intel_dsi_vbt_exec_sequence(intel_dsi, MIPI_SEQ_POWER_OFF);
+		msleep(1500);
+	}
+
 	intel_dsi_wait_panel_power_cycle(intel_dsi);
